WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.200
So, have you ever felt this, like, deeply weird sense of burnout?

00:05.460 --> 00:06.580
Oh, yeah. Constantly.

00:06.720 --> 00:12.200
Right. And I don't mean just, you know, the kind of tired you get after a long run or a really tough week at the office.

00:12.560 --> 00:20.300
I am talking about this profound, almost lingering exhaustion from just having to constantly optimize yourself.

00:20.340 --> 00:21.540
Yeah, the optimization trap.

00:21.600 --> 00:22.820
Exactly. Like, think about it.

00:22.820 --> 00:30.380
You're out there managing your personal brand, curating this flawless LinkedIn profile, trying to, you know, biohack your sleuth.

00:30.400 --> 00:31.500
Tracking every single metric.

00:31.760 --> 00:42.320
Tracking it all. Optimizing your morning routine with supplements, getting the perfect sunlight exposure, and all of this while trying to project this image of just, well, perfect, frictionless productivity.

00:42.740 --> 00:47.160
It really does feel like you are running a PR firm and the only client is you.

00:47.160 --> 00:58.580
Yes. That is exactly it. And if you have felt that gnawing sense that you are polishing this version of yourself while the actual physical you is just completely drained, well, you are not alone.

00:58.760 --> 00:59.240
Not at all.

00:59.380 --> 01:05.080
And you are definitely not crazy because there is this deeply rooted structural reason for it.

01:05.080 --> 01:18.280
Yeah, it's entirely systemic. And frankly, the fact that so many people internalize that exhaustion as some kind of personal failure that is literally part of the architecture of the problem we're looking at today.

01:18.360 --> 01:20.620
Which brings us to the mission for this deep dive.

01:20.820 --> 01:21.060
Right.

01:21.180 --> 01:27.820
We are exploring this truly fascinating, incredibly dense paper that came out in June 2026.

01:28.080 --> 01:29.780
By the independent researcher Fliction.

01:30.000 --> 01:32.880
Right. Fliction. And it's titled The Projection Crisis.

01:33.260 --> 01:34.140
It is quite the read.

01:34.140 --> 01:48.140
It really is. So our goal here is to connect the dots between that deep personal exhaustion you might be feeling, the rapid rise of AI in the workplace, and the specific ideological beliefs of billionaire techno-utopians.

01:48.800 --> 01:51.020
And ultimately the future of human law itself.

01:51.180 --> 01:59.420
Yeah, we're taking all these seemingly separate threads and looking at how they are in reality the exact same crisis, just operating at different scales of society.

01:59.420 --> 02:09.200
I mean, the synthesis in Fliction's paper is just staggering because it gives us this unified vocabulary for things we usually treat as completely separate issues.

02:09.360 --> 02:10.960
Right. So where do we start?

02:11.260 --> 02:19.760
Well, the central premise, like the core concept we really need to anchor ourselves to before we wade into the deep end is something the paper calls projection supremacy.

02:20.160 --> 02:21.100
Projection supremacy.

02:21.100 --> 02:35.740
Yeah. And to put it in its most basic form, this is the phenomenon where society starts replacing real, complex, messy human beings with compressed, easily measurable representations of them.

02:35.740 --> 02:40.020
So it's basically the classic philosophical problem, right? The map replacing the territory.

02:40.120 --> 02:43.680
Yes, exactly. But weaponized by modern computation.

02:43.880 --> 02:44.400
Oh, wow.

02:44.560 --> 02:52.560
Right. So the map is the projection and the territory is the actual human life, which the paper refers to in this mathematical sense as your trajectory.

02:52.560 --> 02:54.380
Okay. Trajectory. I like that.

02:54.440 --> 03:01.260
Think of a real human being as this high dimensional, completely unpredictable trajectory moving through time.

03:01.400 --> 03:04.960
You possess tacit knowledge that you can't easily put into words.

03:05.160 --> 03:06.600
You have emotional depth.

03:06.780 --> 03:12.520
You have these wild internal contradictions and latent potential that hasn't even been realized yet.

03:12.600 --> 03:14.320
Basically all the stuff that makes us human.

03:14.320 --> 03:28.360
Exactly. But large institutions, whether that is a multinational corporation or a social media platform's algorithmic feed or a global financial market, they cannot process all of that high dimensional complexity.

03:28.460 --> 03:29.620
It's just too much data.

03:29.800 --> 03:32.500
It is too expensive and way too difficult to compute.

03:32.640 --> 03:35.820
So to make you legible to them, they compress you.

03:36.080 --> 03:37.080
Like zipping a file.

03:37.080 --> 03:40.420
Right. They take a 2D projection of a 3D object.

03:40.540 --> 03:40.740
Yeah.

03:40.900 --> 03:46.760
Your credit score, your engagement metrics, your resume, your quarterly economic output.

03:46.900 --> 03:50.520
I mean, a projection is useful, right? Because it fits into a spreadsheet.

03:50.680 --> 03:58.040
Like I can't put your latent human potential into an Excel cell, but I can totally put your lines of code written per hour into one.

03:58.160 --> 03:59.860
Yeah. The utility isn't the problem at all.

03:59.860 --> 04:05.860
The crisis this projection supremacy occurs when the institution suffers a kind of collective amnesia.

04:05.860 --> 04:07.040
Oh, so.

04:07.560 --> 04:10.420
It forgets that the projection is just a low resolution map.

04:11.120 --> 04:14.600
And it starts treating that projection as the actual complete territory.

04:14.780 --> 04:15.280
Oh, I see.

04:15.560 --> 04:21.320
The system begins to govern and optimize strictly for the representation, completely losing sight of the underlying human reality.

04:21.580 --> 04:25.980
So it optimizes for the credit score, not the actual financial health of the family.

04:26.160 --> 04:31.660
Exactly. Or it optimizes for the engagement metric, not the psychological well-being of the user.

04:31.660 --> 04:41.160
Okay. Before we look at how this plays out in our own minds and in, you know, the AI labor market, I really want to nail down the paper's philosophical North Star.

04:41.360 --> 04:42.440
Yeah, that's crucial.

04:42.660 --> 04:50.280
Because if we are doing everything wrong by optimizing for these projections, these metrics and market outputs, what is the alternative?

04:50.280 --> 04:53.820
What should a healthy society actually be optimizing for?

04:54.100 --> 05:02.160
So affliction argues vehemently against our current default, which is optimizing for immediate economic utility or productivity.

05:02.480 --> 05:02.600
Right.

05:03.000 --> 05:06.600
Instead, the framework insists we must optimize for accessibility.

05:06.960 --> 05:07.480
Now, hold on.

05:07.600 --> 05:07.760
Yeah.

05:07.760 --> 05:12.480
When the average person hears accessibility, we immediately think of physical infrastructure, right?

05:12.720 --> 05:18.400
Like wheelchair ramps, screen readers for the visually impaired, making sure buildings are ADA compliant.

05:18.640 --> 05:19.880
Which is super important, of course.

05:20.040 --> 05:20.560
Absolutely.

05:21.180 --> 05:24.920
But affliction is pulling this term from dynamical systems theory, right?

05:24.980 --> 05:27.840
It's a much broader, almost physics-based definition.

05:28.380 --> 05:28.580
Yeah.

05:28.680 --> 05:32.740
It is a concept rooted deeply in physics and evolutionary biology.

05:32.740 --> 05:44.560
So, in the context of dynamical systems, accessibility is defined as the volume of future options or paths available to an adaptive system at any given moment.

05:44.680 --> 05:44.840
Okay.

05:45.000 --> 05:46.520
Volume of future options.

05:46.740 --> 05:47.600
Think about it like this.

05:48.060 --> 05:52.800
Imagine you are navigating a maze, but the walls of the maze are constantly shifting.

05:52.960 --> 05:53.740
Sounds like a nightmare.

05:54.180 --> 05:54.460
Right.

05:54.460 --> 06:06.220
But if you are an adaptive system like a human being or a community or a whole society navigating an unpredictable environment, you cannot mathematically optimize for an unknown future state.

06:06.460 --> 06:07.360
Because you don't know what's coming.

06:07.920 --> 06:08.360
Exactly.

06:08.540 --> 06:11.340
You literally do not know what the environment will demand of you in 10 years.

06:11.620 --> 06:17.080
So, the most mathematically sound, valuable thing you can do is preserve your volume of future options.

06:17.240 --> 06:20.560
Because without a wide array of options, adaptation just dies.

06:20.760 --> 06:21.000
Yes.

06:21.200 --> 06:23.600
If we bring that down to Earth, it makes total sense.

06:23.600 --> 06:33.160
Like, if you lock yourself into a single, highly optimized, incredibly rigid career path, say you become the world's absolute best at a very specific type of data entry.

06:33.340 --> 06:38.340
And then the environment changes because, you know, a new software automates data entry.

06:39.180 --> 06:40.000
You shatter.

06:40.280 --> 06:40.620
You do.

06:40.820 --> 06:42.640
You have zero accessibility volume.

06:42.820 --> 06:48.100
You have no other paths to take because you traded all your adaptability for short-term optimization.

06:48.100 --> 06:53.620
Which is exactly why accessibility is the precondition for nearly every other human value.

06:54.180 --> 06:54.260
Wow.

06:54.380 --> 06:56.320
I mean, you can't satisfy your personal preferences.

06:56.620 --> 06:58.100
You can't achieve happiness.

06:58.560 --> 07:03.240
You can't even exercise moral agency if you have no options available to you.

07:03.300 --> 07:04.200
That makes a lot of sense.

07:04.400 --> 07:16.120
So, the central warning of the projection crisis is that by forcing everything to optimize for these compressed, legible projections, our institutions are systematically destroying our volume of accessible futures.

07:16.120 --> 07:20.000
And the terrifying part is that everything looks like it's functioning perfectly on paper, right?

07:20.200 --> 07:20.260
Right.

07:20.440 --> 07:21.560
The metrics look great.

07:21.760 --> 07:24.960
The map looks great, but the territory is just eroding away.

07:25.160 --> 07:25.600
Exactly.

07:25.800 --> 07:28.760
Let's look at how that erosion actually feels to the individual.

07:29.320 --> 07:36.420
Because Flixian dives into the phenomenology of this basically how we experience projection supremacy inside our own heads.

07:36.760 --> 07:38.240
Yeah, this is where it gets really personal.

07:38.520 --> 07:43.760
And to do that, the paper leans on the sociologist Irving Goffman and his idea of the backstage.

07:43.760 --> 07:50.800
Right. So, Goffman developed this dramaturgical model of social interaction way back in the 1950s.

07:50.920 --> 07:51.180
Okay.

07:51.660 --> 07:58.740
And his big observation was that humans always present different versions of themselves depending on the audience.

07:58.940 --> 08:04.460
Like, you act differently in a high-stakes job interview than you do having a beer with your sibling.

08:04.580 --> 08:08.880
Exactly. You are, in a sociological sense, performing a role on a stage.

08:08.880 --> 08:14.460
Which, to be fair, isn't a pathology, right? That's just being a well-adjusted human capable of reading a room.

08:14.720 --> 08:17.220
Right. Context switching is a vital social skill.

08:17.380 --> 08:23.680
But Goffman pointed out that historically, behind all those varied social performances, there was a backstage.

08:24.060 --> 08:24.760
Yeah, backstage.

08:24.920 --> 08:28.060
A private realm where the performer could drop the act.

08:28.620 --> 08:36.020
You could take off the costume, relax, experience deep ambivalence, be grumpy, be totally irrational, and just be.

08:36.240 --> 08:37.640
You didn't have to be coherent.

08:37.640 --> 08:43.140
No. The backstage was a sanctuary where the human trajectory could just rest from the labor of projection.

08:43.480 --> 08:49.820
But, I mean, the digital architecture of the modern world has basically bulldozed the backstage.

08:50.220 --> 08:51.120
Completely flattened it.

08:51.140 --> 08:57.620
It feels like the square footage of that private sanctuary has shrunk to virtually zero.

08:58.440 --> 09:03.020
Everything we do now is potentially visible, recordable, and evaluable.

09:03.100 --> 09:04.180
Yeah, there's no off switch.

09:04.180 --> 09:10.940
Like, you're chilling in your living room, which used to be the ultimate backstage, but you're also curating a photo of your living room to put on Instagram.

09:11.280 --> 09:13.360
Or you're tweeting some random thought you just had.

09:13.440 --> 09:13.740
Right.

09:14.060 --> 09:19.760
And your smartwatch is tracking your heart rate to optimize your sleep score so you can sync it to a wellness app.

09:19.900 --> 09:25.260
You are simultaneously the actor on the stage and the public relations department frantically managing the actor.

09:25.260 --> 09:26.260
That is so exhausting.

09:27.260 --> 09:31.880
And Flitschian argues this is far more than just a cultural annoyance or peer pressure, you know.

09:32.120 --> 09:34.660
It fundamentally restructures human cognition.

09:34.860 --> 09:35.520
Oh, so.

09:35.520 --> 09:39.740
The mental machinery that used to operate freely in the backstagement.

09:41.240 --> 09:51.580
The part of your brain that tolerates uncertainty, that tests out half-baked ideas, that allows for internal contradiction without judgment, it's suddenly conscripted into the performance.

09:51.920 --> 09:54.420
So your private mental life becomes enclosed.

09:54.920 --> 09:55.260
Yes.

09:55.920 --> 10:02.220
You are constantly evaluating your own internal states as if an external audience is watching and scoring them.

10:02.220 --> 10:08.820
Oh, man. I see a direct line here to Eric Frum's concept of the market personality, which I know Flitschian incorporates into the paper.

10:08.980 --> 10:09.960
Yeah, it's a perfect fit.

10:10.080 --> 10:16.640
If the backstage is gone and we are quite literally always on stage, what is the script we are reading from?

10:16.640 --> 10:24.320
Like, Frum observed decades ago that in a hyper-market-oriented society, people learn to treat themselves as commodities.

10:25.120 --> 10:29.980
Your inherent value as a living creature gets completely replaced by your exchange value.

10:30.060 --> 10:30.260
Right.

10:30.260 --> 10:36.780
Selfhood stops being a process of internal organic discovery and becomes a process of external optimization.

10:37.260 --> 10:40.780
So you aren't sitting in silence asking, what is true to my nature?

10:40.980 --> 10:45.680
No, you are asking, what traits do I possess that perform well in the current market?

10:46.200 --> 10:48.420
How can I increase my personal brand equity?

10:48.820 --> 10:54.360
I have to pause here because this is triggering a massive realization for me about the modern wellness industry.

10:54.480 --> 10:55.120
Oh, let's hear it.

10:55.120 --> 11:01.800
I've always struggled to articulate why things that are supposed to be relaxing sometimes feel like a second job.

11:02.720 --> 11:03.040
Right.

11:03.040 --> 11:15.100
Like, you are bone-tired from working a brutal week, so the culture tells you, go to a yoga retreat, write in a gratitude journal, meditate, take a cold plunge, track your macros.

11:15.120 --> 11:15.960
It's a whole checklist.

11:16.100 --> 11:16.580
Exactly.

11:16.920 --> 11:19.920
But you aren't doing those things to actually rest in the backstage.

11:20.260 --> 11:24.260
You're doing them to optimize your recovery so you can perform better on the front stage tomorrow.

11:24.260 --> 11:25.140
Yes, exactly.

11:25.240 --> 11:27.180
It is like a smartphone background app.

11:27.480 --> 11:36.360
Even when you lock your phone, put it face down, and think it's resting, the optimization app is just running silently in the background, pinging the server, and draining your battery.

11:36.880 --> 11:38.480
That analogy hits the nail on the head.

11:38.740 --> 11:40.640
You are never actually powering down.

11:40.820 --> 11:41.040
Yeah.

11:41.400 --> 11:48.700
And Fliction uses this dynamic to highlight a massive category error that modern society is making at just a staggering scale.

11:48.760 --> 11:49.820
Okay, a category error.

11:50.020 --> 11:50.920
Refresh my memory on that.

11:50.920 --> 11:59.580
So a category error in philosophy is when you assign a quality to a thing that cannot possibly possess that quality, like saying a rock is sad.

11:59.780 --> 12:00.000
Right.

12:00.260 --> 12:01.280
Rocks don't have emotions.

12:01.480 --> 12:01.740
Exactly.

12:02.440 --> 12:10.680
In this case, society is systematically misattributing structural field distortions to individual deficiencies.

12:10.880 --> 12:11.100
Okay.

12:11.160 --> 12:13.720
I want to make sure I'm fully grasping the terminology here.

12:13.940 --> 12:16.680
What exactly is a structural field distortion?

12:16.680 --> 12:23.040
A structural field distortion is a macro-level reality that shapes the environment you have to navigate.

12:23.220 --> 12:23.900
Give me an example.

12:24.260 --> 12:27.200
It's impossible housing costs relative to median income.

12:27.380 --> 12:29.660
It's wage stagnation over 40 years.

12:29.860 --> 12:30.220
Yeah, okay.

12:30.540 --> 12:35.960
It's the normalization of a mandatory 60-hour work week just to retain health insurance.

12:36.180 --> 12:36.320
Yeah.

12:36.320 --> 12:38.420
It's the breakdown of communal safety nets.

12:38.600 --> 12:40.920
Those are systemic structural realities.

12:41.020 --> 12:42.580
They're the water we're swimming in.

12:42.580 --> 12:43.060
Exactly.

12:43.600 --> 12:54.000
But the wellness industry and the broader ideology of the market personality tells you that your feeling of exhaustion, your panic, and your precarity are actually just personal individual deficiencies.

12:54.220 --> 12:56.940
You're tired because your morning routine lacks discipline.

12:57.080 --> 13:02.520
You're anxious about being laid off because you aren't doing your gratitude journal and cultivating a growth mindset.

13:02.940 --> 13:04.480
That is incredibly insidious.

13:04.600 --> 13:05.200
It really is.

13:05.200 --> 13:14.900
They are effectively telling you that the economic reality is functioning perfectly, and you are the broken cog that needs to be lubricated with a cold plunge.

13:15.060 --> 13:16.720
Yeah, that's exactly what's happening.

13:16.860 --> 13:23.760
But surely fliction isn't saying that doing a breathing exercise or trying to eat healthy is inherently bad, right?

13:24.060 --> 13:24.800
No, not at all.

13:24.880 --> 13:27.180
And the paper is very careful to make this distinction.

13:27.980 --> 13:34.760
A breathing exercise might be the literal only thing keeping your nervous system from redlining in a toxic workplace.

13:34.760 --> 13:35.060
Right.

13:35.320 --> 13:37.120
The practice itself is totally fine.

13:37.240 --> 13:41.600
The danger is the ideological narrative we wrap around it.

13:42.280 --> 13:46.340
A breathing exercise does not magically make a workplace less toxic.

13:46.440 --> 13:47.200
No, it doesn't.

13:47.280 --> 13:49.300
A gratitude journal does not lower your rent.

13:49.980 --> 13:54.720
When we use these tools as substitutes for structural analysis, we trap ourselves.

13:54.960 --> 13:57.860
We commit to this endless treadmill of self-modification.

13:58.280 --> 14:03.480
Because we are trying to solve a systemic mathematical problem with an individual mindset adjustment.

14:03.480 --> 14:05.500
It's a literal impossibility.

14:05.820 --> 14:07.180
But it keeps us busy.

14:07.640 --> 14:10.780
And more importantly, it keeps us from demanding structural change.

14:11.120 --> 14:20.180
Fliction formalizes this individual trap into the math of the paper, introducing what they call the Elu-Deleu's projection principle, which is theorem one.

14:20.260 --> 14:21.060
Yes, theorem one.

14:21.180 --> 14:23.520
I know we've got some heavy theory here, so ground this for me.

14:23.520 --> 14:26.580
So the theorem builds a bridge between two major thinkers.

14:27.220 --> 14:36.020
First, Jacques Elu, the French philosopher, who warned that modern technical authorities optimize for sheer efficiency, independent of any actual human ends.

14:36.240 --> 14:39.260
The system just wants to go faster, regardless of where it is driving.

14:39.620 --> 14:40.060
Exactly.

14:40.060 --> 14:47.720
And second, Gilles Deleu's, who proposed that we are no longer whole individuals in the modern control society.

14:48.140 --> 14:50.520
Rather, we have become individuals.

14:50.960 --> 14:51.520
Individuals.

14:51.560 --> 14:59.260
Yeah, we are chopped up into collections of measurable data fragments, our credit scores, our consumer purchasing profiles, our keystroke productivity metrics.

14:59.380 --> 15:02.380
Which are the projections, the 2D maps of the 3D human.

15:02.500 --> 15:02.760
Right.

15:03.000 --> 15:05.660
So theorem one mathematically models this relationship.

15:05.660 --> 15:13.940
It states that as institutional pressure for efficiency increases, and as the pressure for the continuous recalibration of the subject increases.

15:14.620 --> 15:18.920
Meaning the pressure on you to constantly update your skills and optimize your wellness.

15:19.240 --> 15:19.480
Yes.

15:19.720 --> 15:26.800
As that pressure increases, the institution's attention asymptotically shifts entirely onto the projection manifold.

15:27.420 --> 15:28.640
Okay, projection manifold.

15:29.240 --> 15:33.440
I assume manifold in this context means the mathematical space where all these data points live.

15:33.440 --> 15:33.840
Yes.

15:34.400 --> 15:36.200
The manifold is the space of the map.

15:36.460 --> 15:47.100
And what the theorem proves is that as this pressure cooker heats up, the trajectory, the actual breathing human being becomes mathematically invisible to the institution.

15:47.400 --> 15:47.500
Wow.

15:47.760 --> 15:53.440
The institution continues to govern, and it does so with terrifying efficiency, but it is governing the map.

15:53.620 --> 15:55.720
It is completely blind to the territory.

15:55.720 --> 16:06.400
So if this wellness optimization, this relentless polishing of our market personality, is a trap that just makes us complicit in our own invisibility, how do we break out?

16:06.520 --> 16:07.540
That's the big question.

16:07.560 --> 16:14.120
Because realizing that the system doesn't see you, and that your exhaustion is structural, could easily push someone into total despair.

16:14.260 --> 16:16.720
Like, how do we look at this without just giving up?

16:16.940 --> 16:21.620
This is where Fliction introduces the necessary antidote, a concept called brutal realism.

16:21.900 --> 16:25.020
I really love the edge that term has. Brutal realism.

16:25.020 --> 16:25.940
Yeah, grab you.

16:26.040 --> 16:29.740
But I also appreciate that the paper puts guardrails around it.

16:30.440 --> 16:35.100
Brutal realism isn't just an excuse to be a cynic, and it certainly isn't nihilism.

16:35.340 --> 16:40.000
It's defined as an epistemic stance of accurate causal attribution.

16:40.440 --> 16:43.600
It might help to look at what brutal realism actually opposes.

16:43.820 --> 16:44.600
Okay, let's do that.

16:44.740 --> 16:46.640
On one side, you have toxic optimism.

16:47.480 --> 16:50.360
Optimism assumes everything can be fixed with the right attitude.

16:50.360 --> 16:56.480
It treats heavy structural constraints as if they're just temporary illusions you can think your way out of.

16:56.700 --> 16:58.700
Right. Just smile and visualize success.

16:58.840 --> 16:59.160
Exactly.

16:59.520 --> 17:02.500
And on the exact opposite end of the spectrum, you have cynicism.

17:03.280 --> 17:11.860
Cynicism looks at those same structural constraints and assumes nothing can ever be fixed, which basically converts accurate perception into total passivity.

17:12.060 --> 17:14.320
So brutal realism rejects both.

17:14.320 --> 17:18.360
It does. It begins with honest, unflinching, accurate description.

17:19.020 --> 17:23.200
If a situation is completely dysfunctional, the brutal realist says so out loud.

17:23.260 --> 17:24.260
They don't sugarcoat it.

17:24.520 --> 17:35.920
No. If an institution is failing its people, they acknowledge the failure without immediately turning that acknowledgement into a mandate to improve themselves, and crucially, without giving up their agency.

17:35.920 --> 17:40.480
There is a brilliant point in the text about the cognitive load of positivity.

17:40.900 --> 17:41.260
Oh, yes.

17:41.420 --> 17:52.580
We usually think of optimism as this light, airy, helpful trait, but forced optimism, especially in a broken system, is incredibly heavy.

17:52.580 --> 18:09.980
It is neurologically exhausting. Think about the friction. If you are committed to a narrative that the system is fundamentally fair, and that your personal struggles are just minor mindset issues you need to overcome well, reality is going to constantly assault you with evidence against that narrative.

18:10.180 --> 18:15.240
You will constantly experience systemic friction that just doesn't make sense within your optimistic framework.

18:15.240 --> 18:24.760
Right. And to maintain your forced positivity, you have to continuously explain away all that contradictory evidence. You have to carry an immense cognitive burden to keep the illusion alive.

18:24.860 --> 18:26.920
You essentially end up gaslighting yourself daily.

18:27.260 --> 18:34.320
I'm not burned out because my department laid off three people and gave me their workload. I'm burned out because I didn't drink my 64 ounces of water today.

18:34.440 --> 18:40.680
You have to invent these absurd micro-failures to protect the macro-system from your own critique.

18:40.920 --> 18:45.200
That's wild. But brutal realism simply puts down that burden.

18:45.240 --> 18:54.780
Yes. It allows the residual error, the massive gap between what the system promises, and what it actually delivers to remain visible.

18:55.320 --> 19:01.440
So you simply look at the situation and say, the workload demanded by this corporation exceeds human biological limits.

19:01.920 --> 19:07.020
My exhaustion is the expected, rational, and mathematically correct consequence of this system.

19:07.300 --> 19:10.140
Just saying that out loud feels like a weight lifting off your chest, doesn't it?

19:10.140 --> 19:11.000
It really does.

19:11.000 --> 19:19.360
It really does. And this epistemic stance isn't just a new internet trend. The paper roots brutal realism in some deeply established classical traditions.

19:19.620 --> 19:26.620
It's woven throughout human history whenever people face overwhelming systemic structures. Stoicism is a prime example.

19:26.740 --> 19:33.420
It insists on rigorously distinguishing between what is within your control and what is definitively not.

19:33.420 --> 19:39.020
The stoic doesn't pretend the storm isn't happening. They focus on the integrity of the ship.

19:39.020 --> 19:40.620
And in Buddhism, too, right?

19:40.700 --> 19:48.180
Yes. Buddhism analyzes human suffering largely as a result of creating for conditions to be other than they actually are.

19:48.720 --> 19:51.540
It's the refusal to accept the reality of the present moment.

19:51.640 --> 19:53.240
Then you have the existentialists.

19:53.400 --> 20:00.560
They demand we face the actual, concrete conditions of our existence, rather than hiding behind consoling societal myths.

20:00.560 --> 20:04.520
The paper also cites psychologists like James Hollis and Anthony DeMello.

20:04.700 --> 20:14.080
Right. They spent their careers demonstrating how unnecessary psychological suffering comes from our stubborn refusal to relinquish fantasies about how life ought to be.

20:14.220 --> 20:16.360
We have to drop the fantasy of the fair system.

20:16.500 --> 20:16.900
We do.

20:17.120 --> 20:21.880
If you correctly identify that a problem is structural, you stop the self-blame.

20:22.440 --> 20:26.460
And from that point of clarity from brutal realism, you can make real choices.

20:26.960 --> 20:29.560
You regain your accessibility, your volume of futures.

20:29.560 --> 20:37.420
Maybe you realize you can't fix the job, so you just aggressively renegotiate your personal boundaries and stop caring so much.

20:37.840 --> 20:43.620
Maybe you engage in collective action or unionize with others who share the structural burden.

20:43.900 --> 20:46.880
Or maybe you leave the industry entirely.

20:47.360 --> 20:54.140
But none of those paths are available to you if you misdiagnose the cause of the pain as your own lack of optimization.

20:54.980 --> 20:59.180
Brutal realism restores your epistemic contact with reality.

20:59.560 --> 21:05.800
It is the absolute necessary first step to reclaiming your trajectory from the projection.

21:06.320 --> 21:09.340
You cannot navigate the territory if you refuse to look up from the map.

21:09.480 --> 21:09.880
Right on.

21:10.020 --> 21:11.840
Okay. So we've established the mental baseline.

21:12.000 --> 21:17.260
We know how it feels to have our human complexity compressed into these hyper-legible market personalities.

21:17.260 --> 21:23.600
But if brutal realism requires us to look at the macro structures, we have to look at the most massive structural shift happening right now.

21:23.680 --> 21:24.200
The big one.

21:24.200 --> 21:29.100
What happens when technology gets smart enough to not just read those compressions, but to mimic them?

21:29.320 --> 21:33.520
And that pivot brings us to the political economy of artificial intelligence.

21:33.520 --> 21:42.800
And to frame this, Fliction draws heavily on a dialogue between the researcher Richard Hames and Garrison Lovely, who wrote a deeply researched book called Obsolete.

21:43.140 --> 21:43.400
Okay.

21:43.400 --> 21:49.420
Through their work, the paper arrives at theorem three, projection extraction or projection replacement.

21:49.420 --> 21:52.860
This theorem fundamentally shifted how I think about AI.

21:53.560 --> 22:01.040
Because the prevailing narrative, whether you are terrified of it or excited by it, is that AI is going to replace human beings.

22:01.280 --> 22:02.540
Right. That's what everyone says.

22:02.540 --> 22:07.440
But Fliction points out that from a metaphysical standpoint, that is completely inaccurate.

22:07.920 --> 22:13.040
AI is not replacing humans. It is automating the economic projections of humans.

22:13.260 --> 22:17.200
This is a vital distinction, and it goes right back to our map and territory concept.

22:17.340 --> 22:17.540
Right.

22:17.840 --> 22:24.720
The institution, the multinational corporation that employs you, never actually interacted with your full human trajectory to begin with.

22:24.740 --> 22:25.880
They didn't hire your soul?

22:26.280 --> 22:26.520
No.

22:26.660 --> 22:31.040
They didn't hire your childhood memories, your moral compass, or your tacit wisdom.

22:31.040 --> 22:35.460
They interacted exclusively with your economically useful manifold.

22:35.960 --> 22:43.060
They interacted with the marketing copy you generated, the code you debugged, the customer service tickets you closed, the spreadsheets you updated.

22:43.300 --> 22:44.740
Exactly. They hired the projection.

22:45.040 --> 22:47.320
Right. So the AI doesn't need to be sentient.

22:47.460 --> 22:53.300
It doesn't need to have a consciousness or feelings or a human lifespan to replace you economically.

22:53.620 --> 22:57.480
It just needs to perfectly mimic your economic utility.

22:57.480 --> 23:03.500
The large language models we have today are trained against the projection, not against the human being.

23:03.640 --> 23:06.400
They ingest the text, the code, the outputs.

23:06.720 --> 23:06.840
Yeah.

23:06.960 --> 23:16.120
If an AI can approximate your specific economic projection at a fraction of the cost, you become economically obsolete to that specific institution.

23:16.400 --> 23:20.280
But you, the human being, the trajectory do not disappear into thin air.

23:20.400 --> 23:21.480
No, you still exist.

23:21.680 --> 23:21.840
Yeah.

23:21.840 --> 23:26.300
You still need to pay rent, buy groceries, access health care, and find meaning.

23:26.620 --> 23:29.120
And this is why the crisis is so incredibly acute.

23:29.760 --> 23:40.300
We are automating the precise interface that society currently uses to allocate survival resources, wage labor, without eliminating the human beings who rely on those resources to live.

23:40.380 --> 23:41.500
It's a massive disconnect.

23:41.500 --> 23:46.820
When you look at the public discourse trying to make sense of this, it is deeply fractured.

23:47.340 --> 23:51.360
Fliction categorizes the AI discourse into three main camps.

23:51.620 --> 23:52.420
Let's break those down.

23:52.500 --> 23:53.260
You have the boosters.

23:53.520 --> 23:59.520
These are mostly the tech CEOs and venture capitalists who want to build the models as fast as physically possible.

23:59.920 --> 24:07.260
Their assumption is that the mass of wealth generated will somehow trickle down and everyone will be magically enriched by a post-scarcity economy.

24:07.540 --> 24:07.900
Right.

24:07.900 --> 24:09.340
Then you have the critics.

24:09.740 --> 24:12.440
They argue that it's largely a hyped-up bubble.

24:12.740 --> 24:20.040
They point out the hallucinations, the massive energy costs, and argue the tech isn't actually capable of replacing high-level human reasoning.

24:20.040 --> 24:25.220
But Garrison Lovely introduces a crucial third category, the warriors.

24:26.140 --> 24:29.940
And Fliction aligns the paper's framework with this group.

24:30.180 --> 24:31.340
What do the warriors believe?

24:31.600 --> 24:37.040
The warriors accept that AI capabilities are genuinely advancing and crossing significant thresholds.

24:37.900 --> 24:43.280
But they view that rapid advance as socially explosive under our current institutional structures.

24:43.620 --> 24:45.140
They aren't getting bogged down in philosophy.

24:45.620 --> 24:45.940
Exactly.

24:46.240 --> 24:48.140
They don't focus on whether the AI is conscious.

24:48.280 --> 24:50.200
They focus entirely on the political economy.

24:50.740 --> 24:59.940
Because whether AI is slightly overhyped or genuinely approaching artificial general intelligence is almost a secondary distraction.

25:00.240 --> 25:01.520
So what's the real issue?

25:01.520 --> 25:09.680
The real material question is who owns the computational capability and who captures the massive productivity gains?

25:09.680 --> 25:16.500
And the central variable here, the invisible force that actually dictates how this plays out for the average person, is bargaining power.

25:16.720 --> 25:17.940
Yes, bargaining power.

25:18.260 --> 25:21.440
I found this to be the most chilling part of the economic analysis.

25:22.000 --> 25:28.560
The paper makes the mathematical case that AI doesn't even need to replace a single worker to completely disrupt the labor market.

25:28.760 --> 25:30.880
It just needs to exist as a credible threat.

25:31.240 --> 25:36.080
The credible threat of automation functions as a severe labor market intervention all on its own.

25:36.240 --> 25:36.680
How so?

25:36.680 --> 25:39.700
Imagine you are negotiating a salary increase.

25:40.320 --> 25:46.280
Historically, your leverage was the cost and difficulty of finding and training another human to do your job.

25:46.320 --> 25:47.300
I have leverage.

25:47.300 --> 25:58.980
But if your boss could conceivably replace a significant portion of your department's workflow with an AI model, even if they haven't actually implemented it yet, your leverage evaporates.

25:59.320 --> 26:03.520
The mere existence of the capability acts as a permanent ceiling on wage demands.

26:03.520 --> 26:09.380
It shifts bargaining power away from labor and permanently consolidates it within capital.

26:09.640 --> 26:16.740
Let me play devil's advocate here because the tech industry pushes back on this doom and gloom narrative by talking about the centaur phase.

26:16.860 --> 26:18.520
Ah, the centaur phase.

26:18.520 --> 26:20.000
I hear this term everywhere.

26:20.520 --> 26:22.920
The idea is that AI won't replace you.

26:23.020 --> 26:27.240
You'll just become a centaur, a mythical hybrid of human and machine.

26:27.240 --> 26:34.260
You supply the nuanced human judgment and creativity, and the AI acts as your tireless intern doing the grunt work.

26:34.800 --> 26:38.280
It sounds like a utopian compromise where everyone just gets more productive and happier.

26:38.380 --> 26:38.600
Right.

26:38.600 --> 26:40.080
It is a very comforting narrative.

26:40.580 --> 26:45.420
But Flixman warns that mathematically, the centaur phase is not a stable equilibrium.

26:45.980 --> 26:47.580
It is merely a transition zone.

26:47.760 --> 26:48.580
Why isn't it stable?

26:48.820 --> 26:51.040
Think about what actually happens in the centaur phase.

26:51.600 --> 26:58.700
The portion of your job that the AI cannot do shrinks continuously over time as the models update.

26:58.940 --> 27:05.820
Every year, the AI handles a bit more of the reasoning, a bit more of the drafting, a bit more of the synthesis.

27:05.820 --> 27:14.440
And as your unique human contribution to the economic output shrinks, your bargaining power declines in exact proportion to it.

27:14.620 --> 27:14.840
Wow.

27:15.180 --> 27:17.380
Wage suppression doesn't wait for full automation.

27:17.740 --> 27:21.140
It proceeds ruthlessly throughout the entire centaur phase.

27:21.220 --> 27:22.260
That makes total sense.

27:22.420 --> 27:31.700
The economic incentive for the corporation to finish the automation process and eliminate the human salary entirely only intensifies as they get closer to the goal line.

27:31.700 --> 27:40.100
It's like you're actively training your own replacement, smoothing out the data for the model and cheering for it because it makes your Tuesday afternoon slightly easier in the short term.

27:40.700 --> 27:41.180
But hold on.

27:41.440 --> 27:46.120
Lovely also points out something bizarre about the AI industry's current economic reality.

27:46.760 --> 27:51.100
They are spending hundreds of billions of dollars on data centers, compute power and energy.

27:51.720 --> 27:55.840
And most of these companies are operating at massive, historic financial losses.

27:55.840 --> 27:57.380
They are bleeding money.

27:57.720 --> 28:02.680
This is what gives the critics ammunition to say, see, it's just a VC subsidy bubble.

28:03.340 --> 28:06.960
It looks like a bubble if you analyze it using traditional quarterly profit metrics.

28:07.100 --> 28:07.300
Yeah.

28:07.560 --> 28:13.300
But Fliction notes that this is a recognized historical pattern of aggressive infrastructure capture.

28:13.480 --> 28:14.040
Like what?

28:14.440 --> 28:20.560
Akin to the building of the railroads or the early internet backbone, just on a much more concentrated scale.

28:20.560 --> 28:26.140
The tech monopolies aren't trying to make a 10% margin on software subscriptions next quarter.

28:26.260 --> 28:27.900
They are playing a much longer game.

28:28.280 --> 28:32.420
They're building productive capacity at a scale no one else on Earth can match.

28:33.200 --> 28:35.520
The stated goal isn't software dominance.

28:36.460 --> 28:40.880
The goal is the eventual automation of all high margin cognitive labor.

28:40.880 --> 28:51.840
The paper even cites OpenAI's own mission statements, which explicitly target the creation of artificial general intelligence that outperforms humans at most economically valuable work.

28:51.940 --> 28:52.680
They aren't hiding it.

28:53.000 --> 29:03.820
No, they are explicitly telling the public that their target market is the total share of the global economy currently allocated to human wages, which is roughly $50 trillion annually.

29:04.200 --> 29:09.520
They are attempting to capture the entire labor share of the planet and convert it into compute rent.

29:09.520 --> 29:17.140
And this terrifying trajectory is what leads to Garrison Lovely's counterproposal, which he calls AI reform.

29:17.440 --> 29:17.660
Yes.

29:18.160 --> 29:22.400
The argument here is vital because it avoids the trap of being a Luddite.

29:22.640 --> 29:22.940
Right.

29:23.060 --> 29:29.280
Because the underlying deep learning technology, the neural networks, the transformers, it is genuinely powerful.

29:29.800 --> 29:32.080
It is a monumental scientific achievement.

29:32.300 --> 29:33.440
The problem isn't the math.

29:33.920 --> 29:38.720
The problem is the institutional incentive structure pointing that math at the wrong targets.

29:38.720 --> 29:41.740
The AlphaFold example perfectly illustrates this.

29:41.800 --> 29:43.260
It was such an illuminating contrast.

29:43.760 --> 29:45.060
Let's unpack what happened there.

29:45.460 --> 29:56.460
So DeepMinds, the AI lab owned by Google, developed a system called AlphaFold using the exact same class of deep learning techniques that power the language models trying to automate copywriting.

29:56.720 --> 29:57.060
Okay.

29:57.060 --> 30:03.060
But instead of pointing that immense compute power at automating human labor, they pointed at structural biology.

30:03.300 --> 30:16.000
They tasked it with predicting the three-dimensional structures of proteins based on their amino acid sequences, which had been a massive foundational unresolved problem in biology for half a century.

30:16.000 --> 30:25.040
Just to give some context here, before this, it could take a PhD student years of painstaking laboratory work to map the physical structure of a single protein.

30:25.160 --> 30:25.500
Years.

30:25.760 --> 30:30.360
And AlphaFold successfully mapped hundreds of millions of them in a fraction of the time.

30:30.700 --> 30:33.420
It is a profound triumph for human knowledge.

30:33.620 --> 30:38.960
It opens the door for curing diseases, designing new materials, understanding life at a molecular level.

30:38.960 --> 30:46.760
But, as Slikin points out, solving one of the greatest scientific mysteries of our time had essentially no effect on the parent company's stock price.

30:46.880 --> 30:50.240
Because mapping a protein doesn't automate high-margin cognitive labor.

30:50.640 --> 30:56.760
It is a profound social and scientific good, but it is not a direct wage-capture mechanism.

30:57.160 --> 31:00.260
The AI reform argument rests on this exact dichotomy.

31:00.260 --> 31:12.960
Through aggressive industrial policy, public funding, and democratic oversight, we could deliberately redirect this immense computational power towards solving intractable physical, social, and scientific problems.

31:13.440 --> 31:17.060
Like climate modeling, material science, disease pathology.

31:17.300 --> 31:17.600
Yes.

31:18.180 --> 31:23.840
Rather than letting venture capital use it exclusively to strip bargaining power from the working class.

31:23.840 --> 31:38.900
So, if this technological shift is mathematically designed by current market forces to transfer $50 trillion from workers to a few tech monopolies, creating mass precarity in the process, a huge question arises.

31:39.180 --> 31:40.160
How do they justify it?

31:40.400 --> 31:40.880
Exactly.

31:41.140 --> 31:44.240
How do the leaders of this industry justify this to themselves?

31:44.380 --> 31:45.560
They aren't cartoon villains.

31:45.740 --> 31:47.420
They believe they are doing the right thing.

31:47.760 --> 31:51.760
How do they sell this immense disruption to the public and to their own consciences?

31:51.760 --> 31:55.060
This is where the paper moves into the ideological justification.

31:55.260 --> 31:56.140
Okay, let's get into it.

31:56.280 --> 32:04.840
To understand the mindset, Fliction turns to the cultural research of Boucher and McGavin, who have done extensive analysis on the ideology of the techno-libertarian elite.

32:04.920 --> 32:05.260
Right.

32:05.540 --> 32:10.480
And what they discovered is a fascinating, almost paradoxical appropriation of literature.

32:10.780 --> 32:11.000
Yeah.

32:11.040 --> 32:12.540
Specifically, science fiction.

32:12.880 --> 32:13.200
Yes.

32:13.500 --> 32:18.240
Specifically, the Culture Series by the late Scottish author I.N.M. Banks.

32:18.240 --> 32:25.640
But for listeners who aren't sci-fi nerds, the Culture Series depicts a sprawling post-scarcity interstellar civilization.

32:26.000 --> 32:27.900
It's a massive, complex universe.

32:28.380 --> 32:32.700
It is governed entirely by benevolent, god-like AIs called minds.

32:33.400 --> 32:39.580
In the culture, there is no money, no coercion, no poverty, no police force, and absolutely no wage labor.

32:40.100 --> 32:43.740
Everyone is free to pursue art, leisure, and self-actualization.

32:43.740 --> 32:48.860
And it is explicitly conceived by the author as a left-wing anarcho-socialist utopia.

32:49.200 --> 33:03.740
Yet, as Boucher and McGavin point out, many of the most prominent tech billionaires' individuals who aggressively advocate for unregulated capitalism, hyper-libertarianism, and the privatization of public goods, frequently cite Banks' Culture Series as their primary inspiration.

33:04.460 --> 33:07.160
And their literal blueprint for the future they are building.

33:07.320 --> 33:09.180
It is a wild cognitive dissonance.

33:09.180 --> 33:09.980
It really is.

33:10.320 --> 33:19.000
Boucher and McGavin point out this dynamic where, you know, regardless of your own political leanings, it's fascinating that a libertarian capitalist framework is adopting a socialist utopian vocabulary.

33:19.360 --> 33:19.540
Right.

33:19.600 --> 33:25.220
The research just highlights how they justify the actions, not whether the politics themselves are right or wrong.

33:25.420 --> 33:25.740
Exactly.

33:25.960 --> 33:29.540
So how does a libertarian capitalist square that circle?

33:30.140 --> 33:34.680
They use a narrative loophole found right in the books, a concept called special circumstances.

33:34.680 --> 33:42.060
In the novels, special circumstances is the elite intelligence and espionage agency of this utopian civilization.

33:42.640 --> 33:51.280
Because the culture is so peaceful and highly evolved, they occasionally run into aggressive, primitive civilizations that threaten them or others.

33:51.280 --> 33:55.220
Special circumstances are the operatives who do the necessary dirty work.

33:55.820 --> 34:05.080
They use covert manipulation, regime change, interference, and sometimes outright violence in the present to ensure the survival and expansion of the utopia in the long run.

34:05.460 --> 34:08.500
They are authorized to do bad things now for the greater good of the future.

34:09.020 --> 34:15.520
And Boucher and McGavin argue that today's tech elites have deeply internalized this exact narrative structure.

34:15.520 --> 34:22.240
They view themselves not as everyday businessmen, but as the real-world equivalent of special circumstances.

34:22.780 --> 34:26.780
In political theory, this functions as what Carl Schmitt called a state of exception.

34:27.400 --> 34:27.680
Yes.

34:28.560 --> 34:37.020
The sovereign ruler is defined as the one who has the power to declare an emergency and temporarily suspend normal ethical and legal constraints.

34:37.280 --> 34:43.080
According to this analysis, these tech leaders are unilaterally declaring a civilizational state of exception.

34:43.080 --> 34:57.160
We have to break the rules, we have to disrupt global labor markets, we have to scrape all the world's copyrighted data without permission, we have to bypass sluggish democratic oversight because we are building the utopia.

34:57.800 --> 35:02.420
The glorious post-scarcity future is at stake, so the ends justify the means.

35:02.780 --> 35:08.800
Every present harm inflicted on real people is just mentally relabeled as a necessary transition cost.

35:08.800 --> 35:14.760
Which brings us to how Flixian formalizes this ideology mathematically in Theorem 7, Future Supremacy.

35:14.880 --> 35:15.460
Theorem 7.

35:15.900 --> 35:24.540
This models the core logic of long-termism, which is part of a broader ideological cluster, extremely popular in Silicon Valley, known as Tescreal.

35:24.880 --> 35:29.860
I see Tescreal thrown around on social media, often sounding like an internet conspiracy theory.

35:30.380 --> 35:32.800
But it's a real academic classification, isn't it?

35:32.840 --> 35:33.600
It is very real.

35:33.900 --> 35:37.040
Break down that acronym for us, because it sounds like a pharmaceutical drug.

35:37.040 --> 35:46.700
It stands for transhumanism, extropianism, singularitarianism, cosmism, rationalism, effective altruism, and long-termism.

35:46.920 --> 35:48.060
Wow, that's a mouthful.

35:48.380 --> 35:52.400
While there are nuances between them, they share a core belief system.

35:53.040 --> 35:57.440
That human beings should transcend their biological limits through technology.

35:57.780 --> 36:02.400
That the creation of a super-intelligent AI is our primary cosmic destiny.

36:02.740 --> 36:06.480
And that our moral obligations extend millions of years into the future.

36:06.480 --> 36:07.000
Right.

36:07.360 --> 36:13.460
And long-termism specifically provides the mathematical justification for the state of exception we just discussed.

36:13.640 --> 36:13.880
Yes.

36:14.160 --> 36:18.000
Let's dig into the math of long-termism, because it sounds rational on the surface.

36:18.220 --> 36:19.440
Caring about the future is good.

36:19.840 --> 36:21.020
Caring about the future is good.

36:21.640 --> 36:27.280
But long-termism calculates moral utility by aggregating total value across time.

36:27.700 --> 36:28.940
Okay, what does that mean in practice?

36:28.940 --> 36:33.820
Imagine a theoretical future where humanity successfully colonizes the Milky Way galaxy.

36:34.420 --> 36:43.400
Or where we create Dyson spheres powering supercomputers that host trillions upon trillions of digital human consciousnesses living in simulated bliss.

36:43.500 --> 36:45.000
That's a lot of digital people.

36:45.420 --> 36:57.140
When you calculate the mathematical utility or happiness of that imagined future, the number approaches infinity simply because the population size is so unimaginably vast.

36:57.140 --> 37:08.620
Right. If you have trillions of happy digital people in the year 4000, they mathematically outweigh the mere 8 billion physical humans alive today by orders of magnitude.

37:08.820 --> 37:10.100
And that is the trap.

37:10.740 --> 37:19.200
In the mathematical objective function of a strict long-termist, the weight given to the distant hypothetical future completely dominates the equation.

37:19.200 --> 37:20.900
So what does Theorem 7 show?

37:21.140 --> 37:33.140
Theorem 7 shows that when the future projection is weighted heavily enough, the constraints of the present, the actual suffering, the displacement of workers, the environmental degradation, the poverty of living humans today drop out of the equation entirely.

37:33.320 --> 37:33.780
It's advantage.

37:33.920 --> 37:34.820
They round down to zero.

37:35.080 --> 37:37.640
They become mathematically invisible to the optimizing agent.

37:37.640 --> 37:44.640
The algorithm literally cannot see your suffering because it is blinded by the glare of a trillion hypothetical digital utopians.

37:46.000 --> 37:52.420
This is the exact same structural inversion we saw with wellness culture in segment one, just scaled up to a civilizational level.

37:52.760 --> 37:54.500
It is the identical pathology.

37:55.220 --> 38:02.240
Wellness culture takes a systemic societal harm and relabels it a personal defect so the economic system doesn't have to change.

38:02.240 --> 38:10.740
And techno-utopianism takes a present societal harm and relabels it a transition cost so the corporate trajectory doesn't have to change.

38:10.820 --> 38:16.860
In both cases, the real breathing human trajectory is erased by a projection.

38:17.580 --> 38:19.280
The map eats the territory.

38:19.440 --> 38:20.200
That is profound.

38:20.320 --> 38:25.060
And honestly, it's terrifying because the logic is so watertight once you accept the premise.

38:25.380 --> 38:25.760
It is.

38:25.960 --> 38:28.540
But this raises a foundational question for me.

38:28.600 --> 38:30.480
I want to push back on the premise for a second.

38:30.680 --> 38:31.100
Go for it.

38:31.100 --> 38:33.360
Are all compressions bad?

38:33.760 --> 38:35.340
Are all projections evil?

38:35.880 --> 38:44.020
Is fliction arguing for some kind of anarcho-primitivism where we just abandon all maps, models, and metrics and wander blindly through the territory of life?

38:44.140 --> 38:46.840
Because that doesn't seem viable for a global society.

38:46.960 --> 38:47.420
Not at all.

38:47.440 --> 38:48.260
It wouldn't be viable.

38:48.400 --> 38:51.120
And fliction is extremely careful to clarify this point.

38:51.780 --> 38:55.040
Compression is an absolute necessity for civilization to function.

38:55.220 --> 38:57.600
Language itself is a massive compression.

38:57.600 --> 39:07.000
You take an incredibly complex, multi-sensory emotional experience and compress it into discrete vocal symbols so someone else can understand it.

39:07.320 --> 39:10.720
A scientific theory is a projection that allows you to predict phenomena.

39:11.260 --> 39:16.300
The legal system is a projection that allows millions of strangers to coordinate without killing each other.

39:16.300 --> 39:19.700
So the act of making a projection is not the crisis.

39:19.700 --> 39:26.580
So what distinguishes a functional, healthy projection from the pathological ones causing this crisis?

39:26.820 --> 39:30.440
The difference lies entirely in whether the projection preserves accessibility.

39:30.840 --> 39:32.560
Ah, back to the North Star.

39:32.680 --> 39:33.100
Exactly.

39:33.740 --> 39:34.840
Remember our North Star.

39:34.840 --> 39:39.700
Does the projection expand your volume of future options, or does it collapse them?

39:40.140 --> 39:42.360
So how does fliction formalize this?

39:42.660 --> 39:47.000
By separating all projections into two categories, generators and realizations.

39:47.580 --> 39:51.000
The paper uses an audio analogy here that completely blew my mind.

39:51.100 --> 39:53.060
I think it's the best way to understand this math.

39:53.200 --> 39:53.880
Let's talk about it.

39:53.880 --> 39:57.160
Let's talk about the difference between a vinyl record and a MIDI file.

39:57.440 --> 39:59.660
It's a brilliant way to conceptualize the math.

40:00.240 --> 40:01.880
Think about a traditional vinyl record.

40:01.880 --> 40:05.160
What is actually physically stored on that disc?

40:05.240 --> 40:06.720
It's basically a groove, right?

40:06.900 --> 40:07.160
Yes.

40:07.700 --> 40:10.780
It is storing a single static realization.

40:11.700 --> 40:17.660
It captures one specific waveform trajectory of one specific musical performance in time.

40:18.020 --> 40:20.500
It is a literal physical groove.

40:20.720 --> 40:27.020
And when you drop the needle, you get that exact performance played back to you perfectly, but that is all you get.

40:27.100 --> 40:28.100
You can't change the tempo.

40:28.260 --> 40:29.960
You can't swap out the guitar for a piano.

40:29.960 --> 40:31.900
You are locked into the realization.

40:32.300 --> 40:39.540
But a symbolic system, like a piece of sheet music or a digital MIDI file, a MIDI file doesn't actually store the sound wave at all.

40:39.740 --> 40:40.540
No, it doesn't.

40:40.620 --> 40:41.660
It stores the constraints.

40:41.840 --> 40:44.840
It stores the notes, the tempo, the pitch, the instrument specifications.

40:45.160 --> 40:46.120
It stores the generator.

40:46.240 --> 40:46.680
Exactly.

40:47.180 --> 40:50.520
The realization is a single frozen path through the state space.

40:50.860 --> 40:55.700
The generator is a constraint structure that defines a whole family of possible paths.

40:55.700 --> 40:59.460
With a vinyl record, your only option is to listen passively.

41:00.160 --> 41:11.340
With a MIDI file, which takes up a fraction of the data size, you can transpose the key, you can change the instruments, you can alter the tempo, you can feed it into an algorithm to create infinite jazz variations.

41:11.340 --> 41:18.320
So a smaller amount of data, the generator actually preserves access to a vastly larger space of possible futures.

41:18.600 --> 41:19.780
And we see this everywhere now.

41:20.500 --> 41:24.940
The modern generative AI text-to-image models operate on this principle.

41:25.300 --> 41:33.320
You type a prompt, establishing the constraints, and you create a generator that can produce infinite varied realizations of that concept.

41:33.320 --> 41:37.240
Now take that exact mathematical logic and apply it to human institutions.

41:37.580 --> 41:40.400
An accessibility-preserving projection acts like a generator.

41:40.860 --> 41:43.540
A healthy legal constitution, for example, is a generator.

41:43.620 --> 41:48.380
Because the constitution doesn't dictate every single microaction you must take throughout your day.

41:48.440 --> 41:48.620
Right.

41:48.720 --> 41:57.020
It sets up a broad structure of constraints, fundamental rights, and laws within which massive, wildly diverse human trajectories can flourish.

41:57.200 --> 42:02.340
It establishes the rules of the game so that you have the maximum amount of accessible futures to explore.

42:02.340 --> 42:03.060
I see.

42:03.380 --> 42:05.560
And a pathological institution does the opposite.

42:06.320 --> 42:08.720
It optimizes for a static realization.

42:09.100 --> 42:11.500
It dictates exactly what the output must be.

42:11.840 --> 42:17.280
It says, this specific set of engagement metrics is the only acceptable way to exist on our platform.

42:17.480 --> 42:24.920
Or, this specific quarterly economic output is the only acceptable way to be deemed a valuable human being in our society.

42:24.920 --> 42:36.720
And when an institution enforces a realization, instead of protecting a generator, it causes what Flixing calls trajectory collapse, which is formalized as theorem nine in the paper.

42:36.880 --> 42:37.980
Trajectory collapse.

42:37.980 --> 42:46.440
Think about what happens when your economic utility is extracted and automated by AI, and the institution subsequently withdraws the resources you need to survive.

42:46.760 --> 42:49.680
Your income, your social status, your healthcare access.

42:49.820 --> 42:52.880
Your volume of accessible futures literally shrinks.

42:53.360 --> 42:59.260
Your ability to adapt, to explore new careers, start a family, to take risks, it all collapses.

42:59.260 --> 43:07.600
Your life options vanish because the institution optimized a rigid realization and abandoned the generator that sustains human thriving.

43:07.800 --> 43:10.700
The map ate the territory, and the territory starved.

43:10.920 --> 43:12.380
That's a chilling way to put it, but yeah.

43:12.540 --> 43:15.460
So, we've identified the pathology from the inside out.

43:16.000 --> 43:25.120
We know how it warps our individual minds, how it threatens our economic stability, how it is justified by billionaire ideology, and how it functions on a mathematical level.

43:25.220 --> 43:26.180
We've covered the problem.

43:26.180 --> 43:32.080
How do we cure it? How do we stop trajectory collapse at a civilizational scale before it's too late?

43:32.460 --> 43:35.580
This is where we need to look at the legal and regulatory solutions.

43:35.800 --> 43:42.360
To address the legal layer, Flixing integrates the framework of legal scholars Toxon and Arbel.

43:42.680 --> 43:54.440
Yes, and their very first, most critical intervention is that we have to fundamentally reject the current global narrative surrounding AI development, which they call the arms race frame.

43:54.440 --> 43:56.740
Oh, we hear this every single day in the news.

43:56.800 --> 43:57.300
It's everywhere.

43:57.460 --> 44:03.600
The idea that if we don't build the godlike AI superintelligence first, our geopolitical rivals will.

44:03.720 --> 44:12.440
Or, on a corporate level, if our company slows down testing to make sure the model is safe, our competitors will release theirs and crush our market share.

44:12.440 --> 44:19.660
Toxon and Arbel argue that this race metaphor is not just misleading, it is actively dangerous to democratic survival.

44:20.060 --> 44:25.300
Translating this into Flixing's formal language, the race frame is a one-dimensional projection.

44:25.720 --> 44:26.180
How so?

44:26.180 --> 44:40.780
It takes an incredibly complex, multi-dimensional policy space, which should include options like international non-proliferation treaties, differentiated safety protocols, sector-specific deployment rules, and robust democratic deliberation.

44:41.000 --> 44:46.000
And it compresses all that nuance down to a single axis, faster versus slower.

44:46.000 --> 44:58.500
And if you allow the narrative to be forced onto that single axis, any attempt at caution, any demand for safety testing, or any proposal for regulation is instantly painted as unilateral disarmament.

44:58.560 --> 45:00.160
It artificially limits our choices.

45:00.340 --> 45:03.120
It's the map eating the territory of our democratic process.

45:03.120 --> 45:04.620
That is precisely what it is.

45:05.040 --> 45:16.780
By forcing the decision into a one-dimensional projection, the institution in this case, the government and the public, becomes completely blind to all the other actual viable policy options that exist in the high-dimensional space.

45:17.120 --> 45:19.060
So how do we break out of that 1D trap?

45:19.160 --> 45:22.380
What did Toxon and Arbel actually suggest we do instead of just yelling stop?

45:22.600 --> 45:25.360
They propose a legal framework called adaptive regulation.

45:26.480 --> 45:30.460
Infliction synthesis, this is theorem 8, regulatory optionality.

45:30.500 --> 45:31.420
Or adaptive regulation.

45:31.420 --> 45:45.380
The goal of adaptive regulation isn't to draft a massive, brittle law that tries to rigidly ban specific technologies, because the technology moves too fast, and the law will be obsolete before it's even printed.

45:45.500 --> 45:46.260
That's always the goal.

45:46.740 --> 45:50.700
The goal is to actively use the law to preserve human options.

45:51.340 --> 45:52.520
Remember the North Star.

45:53.240 --> 45:56.140
We want to maximize accessible futures.

45:56.480 --> 45:59.340
What does adaptive regulation actually look like in practice?

45:59.340 --> 46:01.020
How do you write a law that adapts?

46:01.020 --> 46:03.140
You build it using dynamic mechanisms.

46:03.820 --> 46:05.900
One key mechanism is legislative triggers.

46:06.140 --> 46:06.600
Okay, trigger.

46:06.800 --> 46:13.000
Instead of trying to guess what a language model will be capable of in five years, you pass a law today that establishes conditional thresholds.

46:13.020 --> 46:13.580
Give me an example.

46:13.580 --> 46:38.060
The law says if at any point an AI model crosses this specific measurable capability threshold, for example, the demonstrated ability to autonomously write code that hacks critical water infrastructure, or the ability to mass generate personalized biological weapon blueprints, then these specific severe safety regulations and deployment pauses automatically activate.

46:38.060 --> 46:39.300
I really like that.

46:39.300 --> 46:44.900
It adapts to the reality of the territory as it unfolds, rather than trying to map a future that hasn't happened yet.

46:44.940 --> 46:45.320
Exactly.

46:45.480 --> 46:48.800
It also relies heavily on aggressive corporate disclosure requirements.

46:49.060 --> 46:51.680
You force the developers to open up their black boxes.

46:51.860 --> 46:59.760
You mandate public reporting on training data, energy usage, and internal safety testing failures, so the public and the regulators aren't flying completely blind.

46:59.760 --> 47:13.380
And crucially, adaptive regulation uses sunset and sunrise clauses, meaning the laws automatically expire after a certain number of years, forcing the legislature to actively re-debate and re-authorize them based on new empirical evidence.

47:13.560 --> 47:19.920
Rather than letting some outdated zombie law from the early 2000s dictate the future of artificial intelligence.

47:19.920 --> 47:20.800
Precisely.

47:20.800 --> 47:30.800
All of these mechanisms, triggers, disclosures, sunset clauses, are designed specifically to maintain the volume of admissible future trajectories.

47:30.920 --> 47:34.680
They push back against the accelerationist pressure to close off our options.

47:34.960 --> 47:39.920
But the immediate pushback from the tech companies, the boosters, will be, you're stifling innovation.

47:40.440 --> 47:41.960
You are operating on fear.

47:41.960 --> 47:50.900
You need to wait until there is absolute empirical proof of societal harm before you bring the heavy hand of government regulation down on us.

47:51.380 --> 47:56.040
And this is where Toxin and Arbel stress a concept that is absolutely paramount.

47:56.640 --> 48:03.880
We cannot wait for absolute certainty because the deployment of advanced AI poses a risk of irreversible trajectory collapse.

48:04.100 --> 48:04.620
Irreversible.

48:05.040 --> 48:07.440
There's a fundamental asymmetry in risk management here.

48:07.440 --> 48:15.020
If a harm is reversible, say, a new tax policy slows down economic growth for a quarter, waiting to regulate, just costs you some time and money.

48:15.200 --> 48:16.500
You could always repeal the tax.

48:16.920 --> 48:30.900
But if a harm is irreversible, if an AI system causes catastrophic social destabilization, or if it permanently hollows out the economic agency of the working class to the point where they can no longer politically organize,

48:31.820 --> 48:34.580
then waiting for certainty costs you the futures themselves.

48:34.580 --> 48:39.580
If we lose the volume of possible futures, we can't just hit an undo button and get them back.

48:39.900 --> 48:41.080
The generator is destroyed.

48:41.440 --> 48:46.760
Precautionary regulation in this context is basically brutal realism applied to the legal system.

48:47.080 --> 48:47.820
Yes, it is.

48:47.840 --> 48:59.440
It is looking unflinchingly at the reality of irreversible harm and saying we must legally preserve our options rather than banking the entire survival of the human race on a utopian sci-fi fantasy.

48:59.440 --> 49:12.160
It is the democratic refusal to let the accelerationist projection substitute a single, highly risky, elite, imagined future for the full, vibrant range of what humanity could actually choose to become.

49:12.260 --> 49:12.660
Wow.

49:12.880 --> 49:14.780
Okay, this has been an absolute journey.

49:14.940 --> 49:16.800
We have covered a massive amount of ground today.

49:16.900 --> 49:18.600
Let's synthesize all of this and bring it home.

49:18.760 --> 49:19.260
Sounds good.

49:19.540 --> 49:23.020
We've explored the projection crisis across four distinct scales.

49:23.460 --> 49:27.940
Infliction provides a unified strategy to fight back against the map, eating the territory.

49:27.940 --> 49:28.380
Yes.

49:28.920 --> 49:34.980
To cure the pathology, we need to apply a specific intervention at every single level we discussed.

49:35.340 --> 49:36.700
Starting with the individual level.

49:36.880 --> 49:40.660
At the phenomenological level, the level of our own minds, we need brutal realism.

49:41.480 --> 49:52.340
We have to restore accurate causal attribution, recognize the structural field distortions for what they are, and absolutely start blaming ourselves for systemic economic failures.

49:52.600 --> 49:54.240
We have to reclaim our backstage.

49:54.860 --> 49:57.600
At the economic level, we need AI reform.

49:57.600 --> 49:58.040
Yes.

49:58.500 --> 50:05.940
We have to fiercely protect our material futures by demanding that this incredible technology be steered towards solving real physical bottlenecks.

50:06.140 --> 50:10.080
Like the protein folding of AlphaFold, or clean energy breakthroughs.

50:10.120 --> 50:14.280
Rather than being deployed as a weapon for the mass automation of human bargaining power.

50:14.440 --> 50:18.860
At the ideological level, we need a robust democratic critique of techno-utopianism.

50:18.860 --> 50:29.260
We must protect the living, breathing humans of the present from being mathematically erased by the imagined, hypothetical, digital beings of the long-termist future.

50:29.980 --> 50:31.760
We reject the state of exception.

50:31.760 --> 50:35.280
And finally, at the legal level, we need adaptive regulation.

50:35.860 --> 50:44.080
We need laws that legally preserve human options, utilize dynamic triggers, and help us escape the fatal one-dimensional trap of the arms race.

50:44.080 --> 50:51.000
When you combine all these interventions, you arrive at the paper's final, profoundly beautiful formula for civilizational health.

50:51.160 --> 50:51.920
What's the formula?

50:52.160 --> 50:55.580
Civilizational health equals the preservation of trajectory diversity.

50:55.580 --> 51:03.540
Trajectory diversity, meaning a healthy society isn't a finely-tuned machine designed to perfectly optimize economic projections.

51:03.760 --> 51:04.020
No.

51:04.360 --> 51:14.180
A healthy society is a vibrant, messy, expansive, high-dimensional space that allows for the maximum number of different, viable human lives to flourish.

51:14.360 --> 51:18.360
It is a society that constantly reminds itself that the map is not the territory.

51:18.760 --> 51:20.160
The person is not the profile.

51:20.700 --> 51:22.560
The human being is not the economic output.

51:22.560 --> 51:28.480
And that the future is not a predetermined metric to be optimized, but an open, accessible space of possibility.

51:28.900 --> 51:31.020
I mean, that's it in a nutshell.

51:31.340 --> 51:31.980
I love that.

51:32.240 --> 51:35.480
And that brings me right back to where we started with the feeling of burnout.

51:36.140 --> 51:39.180
I want to leave you, the listener, with a challenge.

51:39.440 --> 51:40.720
Yeah, a practical takeaway.

51:41.080 --> 51:44.100
Think about the last time you heavily optimized a part of your life.

51:44.480 --> 51:49.420
Maybe it was your diet, your calendar, your morning routine, or the persona you project online.

51:49.420 --> 51:52.980
Were you expanding your actual future possibilities?

51:53.500 --> 51:58.340
Were you giving yourself more room to breathe, to adapt, to make mistakes, and to explore?

51:58.600 --> 52:04.200
Or were you just furiously polishing the map while the actual territory of your life slowly burned out?

52:04.200 --> 52:11.560
Next time you feel that immense, crushing pressure to perform your own efficiency for an invisible audience, stop and ask yourself,

52:12.000 --> 52:16.820
are you maintaining your generator, preserving your capacity to create a beautiful, unpredictable life?

52:17.160 --> 52:21.500
Or are you just blindly playing back someone else's static realization?

52:21.860 --> 52:24.320
It's the most important question we can ask ourselves right now.

52:24.380 --> 52:25.000
It really is.

52:25.120 --> 52:28.480
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the projection crisis.

52:29.120 --> 52:31.620
Take care of your territory, and we will see you next time.

